<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nurhaci</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">unified Jurchens <a href="/years/1583">1583</a>–<a href="/years/1616">1616</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nurhaci was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> chieftain who unified the Jurchen clans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> between 1583 and 1616 and laid the foundations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>. Around 1601 he created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eight-banners">Eight Banners</a>, a military and administrative system organising the Jurchen population. In 1616 he proclaimed the Later Jin state, which his successors turned into the Qing dynasty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nurhaci.jpg" alt="Nurhaci" width="306" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%B8%85_%E4%BD%9A%E5%90%8D_%E3%80%8A%E6%B8%85%E5%A4%AA%E7%A5%96%E5%A4%A9%E5%91%BD%E7%9A%87%E5%B8%9D%E6%9C%9D%E6%9C%8D%E5%83%8F%E3%80%8B_1361x1869.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nurhaci was born in 1559 into the Gioro clan of the Suksuhu River tribe and claimed descent from Mentemu, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> headman who lived some two centuries earlier. He grew up as a soldier in the household of the Ming general Li Chengliang in Fushun, where he learned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandarin-chinese">Mandarin Chinese</a> and read the novels <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romance-of-the-three-kingdoms">Romance of the Three Kingdoms</a> and Water Margin, from which he learned all he knew of Chinese military and political strategy.</p><p>In 1582 a rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> chief, Nikan Wailan, led Ming forces against Fort Gure, and Nurhaci's grandfather Giocangga and father Taksi were killed in the fighting. The Ming returned his father's remains, granted him trade patents and recognised him as Giocangga's successor, but refused to hand over Nikan Wailan. Nurhaci began to build his own power from thirteen sets of armour inherited from his father, attacked Nikan Wailan at Turun in 1584 and beheaded him in 1587.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1593 the Yehe called on a coalition of nine tribes against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jianzhou-jurchens">Jianzhou Jurchens</a>, and Nurhaci defeated it at the Battle of Gure. From 1599 to 1618 he campaigned against the four Hulun tribes, conquering the Hada in 1603, Hoifa in 1607 and Ula in 1613, and finally the Yehe at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sarhu">Battle of Sarhu</a> in 1619. In 1599 he had two translators, Erdeni Baksi and G'ag'ai Jargūci, adapt the Mongolian script to write <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a>. In 1606 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> granted him the title of Kundulun Khan.</p><p>In 1616 he declared himself khan and founded the Jin dynasty, called the Later Jin after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchen-jin-dynasty">Jurchen Jin dynasty</a> of the 12th century. He appointed five trusted companions as chief councilors. He consolidated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eight-banners">Eight Banners</a>, the military system that later formed the backbone of the Qing army. In 1618 he issued the Seven Grievances against Ming rule, most of them concerning Ming favour to the Yehe, and took Fushun, whose commander Li Yongfang surrendered and received a granddaughter of Nurhaci in marriage. His conquest of the Ming region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> prepared the ground for the Qing conquest by his descendants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1626 Nurhaci was defeated by the Ming general Yuan Chonghuan at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ningyuan">Battle of Ningyuan</a>, the first serious military defeat of his life. He was wounded there by Portuguese gunners using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> made at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a>, and he died two days later, on 30 September 1626, aged 67. His tomb, the Fu Mausoleum, lies east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shenyang">Shenyang</a>.</p><p>His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-taiji">Hong Taiji</a> renamed the Later Jin as Qing, and Nurhaci is usually regarded as the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>, with the temple name Taizu. Accounts of his life come partly from later, propagandistic works such as the Manchu Veritable Records and partly from contemporary Korean sources, including the Veritable Records of the Joseon Dynasty.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nurhaci” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Jin also became a model for the Manchus: <b>Nurhaci</b>, a leader of the Jianzhou Jurchen, named his state the Later Jin in 1616 before his successor renamed it Qing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty (1115-1234) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Meanwhile the Manchus, united under <b>Nurhaci</b> and Hong Taiji, pressed on the north-east frontier.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Nurhaci</b>'s Banner system organised the entire Jurchen population into colour-coded units that were at once army formations, census registers and social structures.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Until the end of the 16th century they used a mix of Mongolian and Chinese, until <b>Nurhaci</b> patronised an alphabet based on the Mongolian script and Manchu became the new literary language.</blockquote><a href="/culture/jurchens">Jurchens · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 8th century the Uyghurs adopted it, and from the Old Uyghur script came the traditional Mongolian script and, under <b>Nurhaci</b>, the Manchu script.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sogdians">Sogdians · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Manchu ruler <b>Nurhaci</b> was said to have learned Chinese military and political strategy from it.</blockquote><a href="/work/romance-of-the-three-kingdoms">Romance of the Three Kingdoms · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hetu-ala">Hetu Ala City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in Xingcheng, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.7,124.86&amp;year=1604" aria-label="Hetu Ala City on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7591 678 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Nurhaci"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8041 948h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8041 948h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.7,124.86&amp;year=1604">What was here in 1604? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
